% Twin of RefOwnable (OpenZeppelin Contracts 5.0.2, access/Ownable.sol), translated by the Solidity translator.
% One law per success path of each state-changing function, one integrity constraint per revert path;
% the reverts' reasons are the comments above the constraints. See ownable.ledger.md;
% the contract itself: sources/RefOwnable.sol (File > Show the Original in the Logical English editor, View > The original this was converted from in the LPS2 IDE).
% Who may do what, when, with which effect: the legal view, computed from this program
% (Misc > Legal View of This LPS Program in the Logical English editor, View > Legal view in the LPS2 IDE).

the target language is: lps.

the maximum time is 6.

the actions are:
    *an owner* renounces the ownership; known as renounce_ownership.
    *an owner* hands the ownership to *a new owner*; known as transfer_ownership.

the fluents are:
    the owner of the token is *an account*; known as owner; the zero address by default.

the knowledge base ownable reference model includes:

initially the owner of the token is alice.

% renounceOwnership
% reverts: OwnableUnauthorizedAccount
it must not be true that
    an owner renounces the ownership
    and the owner of the token is an account
    and the account is different from the owner.

when an owner renounces the ownership
    and the owner of the token is an account
then it is not the case that the owner of the token is the account.

% transferOwnership
% reverts: OwnableUnauthorizedAccount
it must not be true that
    an owner hands the ownership to a second owner
    and the owner of the token is an account
    and the account is different from the owner.

% reverts: OwnableInvalidOwner
it must not be true that
    an owner hands the ownership to a second owner
    and the second owner is equal to the zero address.

when an owner hands the ownership to a second owner
    and the owner of the token is an account
then it is not the case that the owner of the token is the account.

when an owner hands the ownership to a second owner
then the owner of the token is the second owner.

% The final state of the scenario below: what the EVM gives for the same calls, and what the twin's run
% under LPS2 reaches (checked by the translator's tests; the calls the scenario marks
% as reverted leave no trace):
%   - no fact of the form: the owner of the token is *an account*
% Run it: Misc > Run in LPS in the Logical English editor, or open it in the LPS2 IDE.
% Who may do what, as queries: the legal view (Misc > Legal View of This LPS Program in the Logical English editor, View > Legal view in the LPS2 IDE).
scenario one is:
    % the EVM reverts the next call (not the owner): the twin's integrity constraints refuse it
    bob hands the ownership to carol from 1 to 2.
    alice hands the ownership to bob from 2 to 3.
    % the EVM reverts the next call (no longer the owner): the twin's integrity constraints refuse it
    alice renounces the ownership from 3 to 4.
    bob renounces the ownership from 4 to 5.

